  <section>
    <div class="callout good">
      <h3>✓ The source-side unlocks landed — the fleet moved from 20 → 35 fully-supported</h3>
      <p>Every build item from rev 2's §04 shipped across <strong>PR #524</strong> (composable auth, OData mode + <code>$metadata</code> discovery, incremental push-down) and <strong>PR #526</strong> (response-decode, async-job, scoped overwrite, <code>_faucet_*</code> metadata columns), plus the reshape transforms (#516) and discovery matrix (#501). Concretely:</p>
      <p><strong>Auth was the biggest unlock.</strong> The composable flow provider (#511 — login pre-flight, credential placement, request signing, OAuth1) cleared <strong>sap, acumatica, bullhorn, skyslope</strong> (session-cookie / dynamic-base-URL / HMAC) from Partial to Supported.</p>
      <p><strong>The two former "transport" blockers are now declarative.</strong> <strong>salesforce</strong> Bulk API (submit → poll → download CSV) is the async-job pattern (#514) + CSV decode (#515); <strong>oracle_fusion</strong>'s base64-XLSX-in-SOAP — the one case rev 2 flagged as WASM-only — is now the response-decode pipeline (#515: base64 → parse xlsx). <strong>No tap requires WASM.</strong></p>
      <p><strong>unpivot &amp; lookup are inbuilt now (#516)</strong>, so the eight "Needs SQL" rows whose only reshape was a wide→long unpivot or a reference-table join (anthropic, sharepointsites, onedrive, bamboohr, hibob, personio, deel, attio) are Supported inbuilt-only. And <strong>scoped overwrite (#518)</strong> decouples the report streams that used to <code>DELETE</code> a window from BigQuery (netsuite fully; quickbooks/xero/zohobooks entities).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="callout good">
      <h3>✓ Round-3 merged (PR #532): the fleet is now 40 / 46 fully-supported, 1 needs SQL, 0 blocked</h3>
      <p><strong><code>tree_flatten</code> (#530)</strong> made the recursive report-tree / Rows-Columns-matrix reshape an inbuilt transform, moving the whole accounting cluster — <strong>quickbooks, xero, zohobooks, rillet</strong> — off the DuckDB SQL path to Supported inbuilt. The only tap still needing SQL is <strong>ukg_pro</strong>, whose sibling-array cross-join is a different shape (a <code>cross_join</code> transform is the tracked follow-up).</p>
      <p><strong>Chained/two-level discovery + collected list dimensions (#531)</strong> made <strong>HubSpot fully supported</strong>: discover object types → discover each type's properties → pull records with the full property list in one request (the runtime custom-object shape). <strong>In-run datetime window slicing (#527)</strong> also landed — bounded/capped date-range APIs (anthropic 31-day, deskera/navan/openai windows) now slice each request as a first-class, resumable unit instead of relying on an unbounded incremental. Plus the staged-load foundation (#528, partial — CH/MSSQL follow-up) and persistent run logs (#529).</p>
      <p>The remaining <strong>5 Partials</strong> are all per-connector vendor specifics, not missing framework primitives: shopify (GraphQL nested-connection paging + ShopifyQL), stripe (vendor SDK + events model), plaid (<code>/transactions/sync</code> + soft-delete), sage (proprietary XML gateway), syteline (report stored-procs).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

